  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1222 PM CDT Sun Oct 4 2026\n\nA broad area of surface high pressure anchored across the \nPlains continues to influence weather across central and \nsoutheast Illinois. A weak, dry cold front will traverse the \nregion today. No cloud formation or precipitation will \naccompany the frontal passage. Following the front, winds shift\nto the northwest around 10 mph. High temperatures today will \nreach the mid to upper 70s under full sunshine. Winds ease \naround sunset as high pressure shifts closer to the Upper \nMississippi Valley. Radiational cooling under clear skies allows\nlow temperatures to drop into the upper 40s/near 50F.\n\nOn Monday, high pressure slides toward northern IL by afternoon,\nestablishing a light northeast wind. Brief post-frontal cold \nair advection caps Monday high temperatures in the low-mid 70s. \n\nGlobal guidance from the ECMWF-ENS and GEFS indicates low\namplitude northwest upper-level flow continuing across the \ncentral United States through the end of the week. Surface high\npressure moves toward the Mid- Atlantic on Tuesday, shifting \nwinds to the south and initiating low-level thermal ridging. \nTemperatures are forecast to warm several degrees above seasonal\nnormals Wed-Sat, with highs generally in the mid-upper 70s for \nnorthern sections of the CWA, to lower 80s south. The dry \nairmass will support comfortable lows in the 50s through the \nperiod. \n\nAfternoon RH will likely fall below 30% for much of the\narea south of I-72 Thu-Fri, which combined with dry fine fuels \nwill increase the risk for field fires during harvest. However,\nwind gusts during this time are forecast to stay below 15 mph, \nwhich should mitigate the risk of rapid spread.\n\nFor next weekend, ensemble and deterministic guidance support a\ntropical system tracking north from the Gulf toward the Ohio\nValley region. Current blended forecast brings a 20-30% chance\nof showers to southeast IL on Sunday. There is a large spread in\ntiming and placement among individual solutions, so we'll need \nto watch this period for potential forecast adjustments.",
